  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 137 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A pleasant day is expected across the region today as \nhigh pressure continues to nose into the region, bringing a period \nof drier weather. Some valley fog that has developed overnight will \ngradually dissipate through the morning, with mostly clear skies and \nsun expected for this afternoon. High temperatures today will climb \ninto the upper 70s to mid 80s. Another day of tranquil conditions is \nexpected for Wednesday as the region remains under the influence of \nridging, with high temperatures a few degrees warmer under light \nsoutherly flow with daytime highs climbing into the 80s areawide. \nOverall, it looks like a few pleasant summer days are on tap for the \nregion.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Heading into the latter half of the week, warm and \nunsettled weather is expected as the region remains sandwiched \nbetween high pressure to the south and an upper level trough to the \nnorth, with zonal flow bringing several different disturbances to \nthe region. With broad southwesterly flow ushering in plenty of \nmoisture, chances for showers and thunderstorms will be possible. \nLatest guidance shows decent instability across the region Thursday \nafternoon, which combined with ample moisture and decent forcing may \nlead to thunderstorm development in the afternoon, with the \npotential for some stronger thunderstorms possible if all the \ningredients align, although details will become clearer as we get \ncloser. Additional rounds of showers and thunderstorms are also \nexpected for Friday. In addition to the shower chances increase \nlater this week, the increased moisture will allow for dewpoints to \nclimb into the upper 60s to near 70 at times, making it feel \nincreasingly muggy and uncomfortable later this week. High \ntemperatures will climb into the 80s, with heat indices climbing \ninto the upper 80s and near 90.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Over the weekend, an upper low will mature on the \nwestern edge of the Hudson Bay while a large western upper high \nflattens out and settles east. This will produce a channeled flow \npattern that commonly sends small scale features racing east in a \nspeedy jet stream. This usually also means that the forecast \npredictability will be low, as coarse resolution models may not \nalways accurately depict these smaller, convectively-influenced \nsystems within the fast flow pattern.\n\nAssessing different ensemble suites, most indicate largely above \nnormal PWATs, although there is a signal for a brief pocket of dry \nair. Some have that dry air in place on Sunday, some have it on \nMonday, and a minority have it on Saturday. So Saturday appears the \nmost likely to feature scattered (even numerous) showers and \nthunderstorms over the weekend. Between Saturday and next Tuesday, \nthere's at least a trough per day passing east, and so there could \nbe some rain for each day. Towards the middle of next week, the \nlarge scale pattern doesn't change too much, but the northern trough-\nsouthern ridge pair shifts east. This will place us a bit more under \nthe influence of the upper low. So after a period of above normal \ntemperatures, we will start trending back towards normal. The \nquestion will be how amplified that upper low remains. Still showers \ncan't be completely discounted given the upper low nearby.",
